 \label{def:hfsens}
Let $f$ be the hazard-free extension of some $n$-input Boolean function. For an $x\in \{0, \un, 1\}^n$, we define the sensitivity of $f$ at $x$ as:
\begin{equation*}
    \sen_\un(f, x) = |\{i \mid \text{there is a $y$ such that $f(y) \neq f(x)$ and $y$ differs from $x$ at, and only at, position $i$}\}|
\end{equation*}
The elements $i$ of the set are called the \emph{sensitive bits of $x$ for $f$}. The \emph{hazard-free sensitivity of $f$}, denoted $\sen_\un(f)$ is the maximum sensitivity of $f$ over all $x$.

A function $f : \zuo^n \mapsto  \zuo$ is \emph{weakly hazard-free} if:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $f$ is monotone wrt the instability partial order.
    \item For all $x\in\zuo^n$ such that $f(y)$ is the same value $b$ for all $y > x$, we have $f(x) = f(y)$ as well.
\end{enumerate}

\label{claim:hfcc_vs_hfdt}
