even when $m=N^{o(1)}$ and the similarity threshold $s \in [m]$ is fixed for all queries.

\label{cor:CC}
There exists a computationally efficient \MA-protocol for Set Disjointness s.t.:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Merlin sends Alice $o(n)$ bits; \item Alice and Bob jointly toss  $o(n)$ coins; 
\item Bob sends Alice $o(n)$ bits.
\item Alice returns \textup{Accept} or \textup{Reject}.
\end{enumerate}
If the sets are disjoint, there is a unique message from Merlin such that Alice always accepts; 
otherwise (if the sets have a non-empty intersection or Merlin sends the wrong message), Alice rejects with probability $\geq 1-1/2^{n^{1-o(1)}}$.

